{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "toolu_01KWN5qXv3NSVPZfhzLLQuxy", "type": "tool_result", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\"excerpt_removed\": true, \"references\": [{\"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_knots\"}, {\"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Archaeology_of_North_Korea\"}, {\"url\": \"https://www.korean-culture.org/eng/webzine/202102/sub10.html\"}, {\"url\": \"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/journal-of-asian-studies/article/stone-knives-in-rice-agriculture-in-korea/224EE5E77BB31D0B15FB0AC7F9F06EC5\"}, {\"url\": \"https://www.koreatimes.co.kr/amp/lifestyle/koreanheritage/20111212/national-museum-of-korea-reopens-prehistory-section\"}, {\"url\": \"https://ggc.ggcf.kr/en/p/60232df17dc7c968444facbf\"}, {\"url\": \"https://www.historynewsnetwork.org/article/119062\"}, {\"url\": \"https://meer.com/en/42135-neolithic-period\"}, {\"url\": \"https://scholar.kyobobook.co.kr/article/detail/4010024969429\"}]}"}]}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-20T23:53:53.065Z", "tool_use_result": {"query": "Korean craft Neolithic stone axes knives tied to waist carry tools maedeup", "results": [{"tool_use_id": "srvtoolu_019neWFkUfyz63VL9eJY1D6c", "content": [{"title": "Korean knots", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_knots"}, {"title": "Archaeology of North Korea", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Archaeology_of_North_Korea"}, {"title": "www.korean-culture.org", "url": "https://www.korean-culture.org/eng/webzine/202102/sub10.html"}, {"title": "www.cambridge.org", "url": "https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/journal-of-asian-studies/article/stone-knives-in-rice-agriculture-in-korea/224EE5E77BB31D0B15FB0AC7F9F06EC5"}, {"title": "National Museum of Korea reopens prehistory section - The Korea Times", "url": "https://www.koreatimes.co.kr/amp/lifestyle/koreanheritage/20111212/national-museum-of-korea-reopens-prehistory-section"}, {"title": "GyeongGi Cultural Foundation", "url": "https://ggc.ggcf.kr/en/p/60232df17dc7c968444facbf"}, {"title": "Paleolithic remains unearthed in Korea", "url": "https://www.historynewsnetwork.org/article/119062"}, {"title": "42135 neolithic period", "url": "https://meer.com/en/42135-neolithic-period"}, {"title": "scholar.kyobobook.co.kr", "url": "https://scholar.kyobobook.co.kr/article/detail/4010024969429"}]}, "The search turned up support for the idea that maedeup grew out of practical knotting, but not a clear, sourced account of Neolithic knots holding stone tools.\n\n- **Origins:** Korean knotting is traced to earlier practical uses of knots, such as in fishing nets, farm tools, and stone knives and axes (Wikipedia, \"Korean knots\").\n- **Waist use:** Wikipedia says the knots were used mainly to hold hunting tools at the waist, initially limited to royalty, though that line carries a \"citation needed\" tag. Its Neolithic passage says knots were then purely practical and tied around the waist, but the excerpt cuts off mid-sentence, so I couldn't confirm the rest.\n- **Korean Culture magazine:** This source places the knot's origins in the Three Kingdoms period (37 B.C.–A.D. 668) and says it was first worn at the waist to carry hunting and fishing gear such as axes and swords. It later became decorative and symbolic.\n- **Neolithic context:** Neolithic Korea (Jeulmun period) saw pottery, settled life, and likely early plant cultivation, but none of the results tie specific knots to Neolithic stone axes or knives.\n\nNote that the Korean Culture source dates the waist-knot practice to the Three Kingdoms period, which is later than the Neolithic. The Neolithic link comes only from Wikipedia's partial, and possibly unverified, passage. A more authoritative archaeological source would be needed to confirm that stone tools were actually carried on knots at that time."], "durationSeconds": 6.168901292, "searchCount": 1}, "tool_result_meta": [{"id": "toolu_01KWN5qXv3NSVPZfhzLLQuxy", "permission_decision": {"decision": "accept", "source": "hook", "reason_type": "hook"}}]}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgEPbR8yP7y1woxbaP6Jt", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "**답: 매듭 (한국 전통 매듭, maedeup)**\n\n근거로 삼은 위키백과 \"Korean knots\" 항목 내용은 이렇습니다. 매듭은 고기잡이 그물, 농기구, 돌칼, 돌도끼를 묶던 실용적인 매듭에서 시작됐고, 신석기 시대에는 허리에 묶어 사냥 도구 등을 지니고 다니는 데 썼습니다. 다만 이 내용 일부에는 \"citation needed(출처 필요)\" 표시가 붙어 있고, 검색 결과에서는 신석기 부분 문장이 중간에 잘려 있어서 끝까지 확인하지 못했습니다. Korean Culture 웹진도 매듭을 처음에 허리에 매어 도끼·칼 같은 사냥·어로 도구를 지니던 용도로 설명합니다. 하지만 시기는 신석기가 아니라 삼국시대로 잡고 있습니다.\n\n검색은 2번 했습니다.\n\n출처:\n- [Korean knots – Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_knots)\n- [Korean Culture webzine (2021.02)](https://www.korean-culture.org/eng/webzine/202102/sub10.html)"}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 1281, "cache_read_input_tokens": 5884, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 1281}, "output_tokens": 450, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-20T23:53:58.135Z", "request_id": "req_011CgEPbQsM8YbtCdquFGMwM"}
